{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/186219-on-snowflakes-and-golden-rules","id":186219,"title":"On Snowflakes and Golden Rules","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-07-02T06:00:58-07:00","id":186219,"title":"On Snowflakes and Golden Rules","content":"I'm a long-time player of the Classic WoD, and throughout my experience with the game the biggest questions I've had were regarding the viability of crossover characters. Abominations, yes, but also things like Cainite Magi (a la Red Sign chicanery), Mokol\u00e9 with Hedge Magic, and Kinain of all varieties using Dark Ages Dominions. Things like, what a Weaver equivalent to a Ferectos would be, aside from just a non-Possessed Drone, or the bigger questions like &quot;was that Mage in the old LARP book actually a Sidereal?&quot; and &quot;how did Genevi\u00e8ve Orseau keep her faerie side?&quot; All throughout the books we find hints and tidbits (or huge craters like Sammy H) that point to greater compatibility.<br /> <br /> I know that the usual answer is &quot;not only no, but hell no&quot; (Time of Thin Blood), what I want to know is why? In the three global Cam chapters I ran with over the years, that was the secret naughty question on everyone's lips. If you indulged the notion, you had done something wrong according to the STs, who got rather angry about it for no good reason. Of course the golden rule would let you play any kind of combination you could think of, but the moment anyone finds out about it, welcome to hell. A lot of people think this would be something cool, but it seems like we don't usually get a voice or a fair discussion. I understand the point of view of the purists who think it should be done a certain way, and I respect that, but I still think about it. What I want to do is figure out what kinds of combinations are theoretically possible and figure out how they would work. If there is already a thread to this effect, please point me to it? Otherwise, I would welcome any suggestions. <br /> <br /> The topics on my mind:<br /> * Mages can be Ghouls, and (I think?) Revenants, but not Dhampir. I don't see any reason for this other than the writer's opinion on what is cool and what isn't. I think a half-vampire mage would be very cool. In fact, that is what I would love to play most of all. Not for the power, but for the concept! A sign of God's forgiveness indeed, if they could join the Celestial Chorus! Or better still, become Imbued. They're already portrayed as vampire hunters all over history and pop culture. D, Blade, etc. They have merits to see through vampiric trickery, even!<br /> * Off Topic: What if a Dhampir could pull a Saulot and become Dhampyr? (KoE: Half-Damned)<br /> * If the Conquistador's Sword was made once, it could be made again. Fera and True Magick. If a fetish can make it available, does that imply availability without said fetish? Primers, Avatar Storm shards? What about the Avatar-Diablerie mentioned in Ascension? If the Fera soul is only half Awake, why not consume one that is fully so? The Red Sign had some neat ideas about Cainites getting their Avatar back (which reminds me of Caine's Awakening and how he then mentioned learning Nine Disciplines, not to mention how close Golconda sounds to Arete), but could that same kind of Avatar-Diablerie serve the same purpose? Under the old system for Sorcerer, Alchemy could create potions to induce Awakening, the First Change, etc. A possible link?<br /> * Hsien Kinain. (Land of Eight Million Dreams.) Welcome to Avatar, the Last Airbender / Legend of Korra. Their reincarnation would seem to debunk this, but elemental infusions are a common enough trope. How could it work?<br /> * Psiads and Yes. One of the Sorcerer editions suggested using Trinity psionics if you wanted to bump up psychic phenomena. Chronologically, if you combined the two settings, Psiads (Aberrant) are around in this time period. What other types could theoretically be Psiads as well? (A Mokol\u00e9 Energetic would be the second of my ideal troupe.) And what was the final ruling on who converts or keeps Numina? One edition said you convert it to new powers, one said keeping it could give you a handy backup power set. Which ruling won out?<br /> * Exalted. The Neverborn are messing around with the Rogue Council missives, that one Avatar spoke of the Usurpation as though it was once a Sidereal, and the Hsien and Wan Kuei falling out seemed to have been over the Usurpation. Oh, and the Scarlet Queen and Ebon Dragon concepts in KoE are killing me. And the Hunter Apocrypha about the Golden Heroes or Shining Ones, whichever it was. I notice the Maelstrom released them at the same time as the Demons, which certainly reminds me of the Jade Prison and how the Unconquered Sun wanted to call the Exaltations back after the Locust Crusade in 1st Edition. (Memory is rusty on all that... I hope I'm remembering that bit right.) So, what happens if an Exalt just didn't die somehow? Sitting out the Third through Fifth Ages in the Panopticon Cataclysmic to observe the world and monitor its stability for argument's sake.<br /> * Semi-Off-Topic: Which reminds me. Dragon Kings. The theory that Dragon-blooded were bred from half-dragon-kings always caught my attention. I don't recall from where I heard that, but I've clung to it for years. A Half-Dragon-King Solar would be amazing. (Or better still, a Sovereign Titanic Exalt who happened to be a Mosok Dragon King fused to the warped prototype Solar Exaltation through a process mirroring Investiture of Infernal Glory and involving an N/A Artifact that could bend the charm's rules a bit to avoid being bound, but still soul-fused in ways normally impossible. I really enjoyed that game...)<br /> * Lastly, why did they limit Hedge Magic to Bastet and Kitsune ONLY with the 20 update? Does it really unbalance a slathering Garou warmachine that much to have Kinfolk powers? And what if a Kinfolk with Numina gets juiced with that serum that sterilizes them but induces the First Change? (Or an Alchemist using a potion of First Change to become a Mokol\u00e9 for example.) Would they then lose the Hedge Magic just because a writer wanted to clarify an old ST fiat? What is the real in-character reason for this limitation? If it's reasonable, I'll abide by it, but so far all of this has just seemed like irritated designers being irritable. <br /> <br /> I hope I haven't ruffled too many feathers by mentioning all of this. Not trying to start a fight, just wanting to brainstorm and gain some clarifications. Maybe I should just make a Shard or something like that, but I really want to be able to speak about this topic from a more informed point of view. Any input is welcome, but please be gentle. <br /> <br /> &quot;I have spread my dreams under your feet; Tread softly because you tread on my dreams.&quot; -- Yeats","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3407-cerushad","name":"Cerushad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3407_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-02T13:42:47-07:00","id":186488,"title":"","content":"(Just responding in no particular order, and only to a few things): <br /> <br /> Half-vampire Mages: It isn't a situation of the writers' thinking &quot;x&quot; is kewl while &quot;y&quot; is laym, but rather their taking the (very correctly so) metaphysical perception that the undead (vampires, and the like) cannot exercise True Magic(k) because they are literally missing the aspect of their soul/metaphysical existence that would allow them to do so (the God-Shard/Avatar/Eidelon/Fount/Sahu/any number of names). They are *dead*, in several mystical ways, and that includes working True Magic(k) (they can, of course, develop potentially any number of static/hedge-based powers and rituals, but they can't channel the raw power of the universe). With the &quot;half-dead&quot; mages (Dhampirs, or Revenants), it's a related case of their souls/essences being bogged/tied down far too securely for the Awakening to ever happen. (For Dhampirs, it's simply impossible; for Revenants, the books literally say it's a one-in-a-whatnot chance, it's so rare, and even if it did occur, said Revenant would eventually corrupt/erode his own soul before he even made it to 40 -- and that's being *VERY* generous, assuming he awakened as a teen and somehow developed Arete:10, had an Avatar of 5+). What some people misperceive about [A]wakening as a Mage (not a mage, but a *M*age) is that it *isn't* like being a wizard in DnD, or what-have-you, wherein the world/reality is so magic-abundant that hedge magic can reach nigh-godlike ability (wizards and such, btw, in most DnD settings are *not* Awakened beings, but &quot;merely&quot; practitioners of very powerful hedge magic; True Magic[k] -- the ability to sculpt/shape Reality(tm), in toto -- is the providence alone of the gods, via Alter Reality or, depending on Edition, similar abilities/delineations). There are far more hedge-wizards in the WoD than there are Truly Awakened Mages; vampires and the half-dead may be the former, but they can never be the latter. (Liches, of course, are the proxy-product of True Magic, and may continue to work such due to taking absolute possession of their god-shards; however, they suffer other side-effects accordingly, most particularly being Paradox prone in the 20th/21st centuries and onward...).<br /> <br /> Sorcerer and &quot;x&quot; : Certain aspects you allude to (such as Sorcerer's possibly being able to create potions/what-have-you's that Awaken others, do this, that and the other) are meant to be *plot devices*, *NOT* something that ever occurs with any regularity or predictability (and, appropriately, are scaled at the mythical 6th dot of Alchemy/whatever). Can there be a Mokole' who practices hedge-magic, or a Kinfolk who does so and then has the Change induced via [fill in the blank with the designated Plot Device power] ? Sure. (Hell, in that context, you *can* go ahead and give yourself the half-dead mage, from the above example -- he won't survive more than a few years or so, but if you don't care about that, then go for it...). But that doesn't mean that such things should in any way, shape or form be routine or even remotely predictable. To tack in another way: Garou, and similar, can *already* work rituals and employ spirit Gifts than simulate &quot;x&quot; effects for &quot;y&quot; reasons -- they don't *need* hedge magic, and in fact such practices may often run counter to the &quot;normal&quot; ways in which they invoke their powers/magic. In certain rare cases such as with the Bastet and Kitsune, those Fera are themselves &quot;left-of-center&quot;, as it were, and represent the &quot;exception that proves the rule&quot;. It's another reason, perhaps, for the Garou to be jealous (or at least envious) of the wisdom/power those two groups possess... with the Garou never realizing that said jealously is part of what blinds them, at least in part, to the possibility of *having* such magics in the first place... #RealFoodForThought<br /> <br /> Linking the two broad concepts above together: Yes -- of course achieving Golconda sounds quite a bit like [A]wakening/what-have-you: it many respects, that's exactly what it is! *However*... that doesn't change the underlying metaphysical elements in play. If said vampire, for example, remained a vampire (albeit a truly enlightened one), then his remaining soul-aspects &quot;awaken&quot;, unify, and allow for him to connect to the broader universe in ways that even Caine/Antidiluvians can't: he doesn't use his blood as often to rise/sustain himself; he can develop his powers to near-godlike levels (up to 10 dots); he is free from induced frenzies; etc.... That *still* doesn't mean, however, that he can become a Mage -- he simply becomes *his* *own* *approximation* of such, given what is left of him, spiritually. On the flip, if a vampire is re-connected to his god-shard (via Osiris, True Enlightenment, &quot;The ST said so...&quot;), then he ceases to be a vampire, and may Awaken to become a Mage. (This, btw, is metaphysically perhaps *THE* most SINGULARLY POTENT thing that could possibly happen in the WoD -- that point CANNOT BE OVERSTATED.) Again, however, you would then have a *Mage* -- *NOT* a vampire-mage. (A few examples like that occurred when Osiris restored to life a number of his followers after All Hell Broke Loose -- a handful of them Awakened back to being mages, again; this, to restate, is however once again a case of PLOT DEVICE making things happen, and the juju involved was truly god-level shit, and even then contingent not only on a god-like being lending his power to the situation, but those affected having spent centuries -- in some cases, millennia -- keeping themselves spiritually/mystically pure enough for such to even have a possibility of occurring... again, PLOT DEVICE-level shit...).<br /> <br /> <br /> You covered a lot of other stuff, and I am on the road w/limited time for posting, so I'll just stick with the above for now. Hope that helps, for what it's worth...<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-02T14:12:14-07:00","id":186501,"title":"","content":"Well, in my opinion,crossover it's tricky. The different game lines have contradictory metaplots, incompatible rules, and, perhaps most important, they have different &quot;moods&quot; or &quot;spirits&quot;. I don't know how to call it.<br /> And you may wat to have a solid crossover universe before doing hybrids, since they implicate crossover. And hybrids need to be balanced, they need rules of their own.<br /> <br /> There are many reasons of why the community of roleplayers it's usually hostile to hybrids. These are some of them:<br /> *They attract powergamers.<br /> *They are complex to ad to a chronicle. And for what?. It's really necessary to play an Awakened Dhampir to portray a sign of God forgiveness?. The mages have their own dramas after all, like a special perception of Reality. If you don't want to play with those, then probably you can do that better with an original house rule rather than adapting mages.<br /> *They are unorthodox <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> .They always appear with labels like &quot;Rare&quot; or &quot;Unique&quot;. So having lot's of them don't reflect the &quot;canon&quot; reality of oWOD. Even if they are &quot;legal&quot; within the parameters of canon rules.<br /> <br /> But then, all those reasons are very subjective. If the Storyteller and players either don't see them as problems or can cope with them, then I don't know why they shouldn't do it.<br /> The Golden Rule it's the right to play your game in any way you and your co-players want. And it should be used accordingly.<br /> <br /> Oh, and ,as far as I know,.Exalted isn't anymore the canonical past of oWOD","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-02-2014, 05:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-02T16:47:18-07:00","id":186619,"title":"","content":"All very helpful information, thank you! I'll take what was said into consideration. The metaphysical reasons behind these inabilities is exactly what I was looking for to either quell or dispel, so I greatly appreciate your input, John. Particularly the part about these things being Plot Device powers. That does in fact put most of my thoughts into perspective. Thank you again. <br /> <br /> The dhampir being mired down by the once-cursed undead essence of its parents definitely puts a stark contrast to Dr. Netchurch's in-character idea that Dhampir are just Revenants done in reverse. That is exactly what I was looking to figure out. Now it makes sense. Thank you!<br /> <br /> One bone of contention I have (more like a pet peeve) is the very concept of powergamers. If I'm playing a character whose concept is to be a normal, well adjusted (or nominally maladjusted) member of an occult race, then the status quo approach is fine. But what about those megalomaniacal sociopaths who really are willing to violate taboo and become powermongers. The character can't be called a powergamer, because to them it's no game, it's their life and their master plan to gain the corrupted powers of the world just like Sammy H did. Especially to an egomaniac who wouldn't see the Skinner as a cautionary tale, but a loser to be one-upped. If the player is just trying to be a jackass and be better than everyone else, sure, that's a powergamer. But even the old Technocracy books had the personality archetype Machine. Regain willpower when you exceed expected potential and show up everyone else. I can appreciate that it's courteous not to play that kind of character in a group, but in a solo game or even a free for all like a sanctioned game. What is the real reason not to pursue power as part of a character concept? I once has a Tremere Diablerist character who legitimately became very powerful, all by the rules, all under the supervision of the VSTs, and once it became obvious that it was working, they forced the retirement of the character. That was unfair. I wasn't even pulling any cross-venue chicanery, all vampire. I earned and they revoked. That's why it irks me so much. Tremere are powermongers by nature, and then as players we are punished for it. Tangent. Anyway...<br /> <br /> I guess the answer to all of my thoughts lies in Plot Devices. I'm immediately reminded of the Sargon Fragment (Giovanni Chronicles). And how quickly it burnt out the vampire who used it, heh heh. Overconfidence. Right, starting to ramble a stream of consciousness, so I'll end off here. Thank you for all the input. If anyone has any more suggestions about limiting factors, possible ways to achieve these things, or legitimate in-character reasons the other stuff can't happen, I'm all ears. I want to make sure I understand all of this before I try to make a case for anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3407-cerushad","name":"Cerushad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3407_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-02T18:41:52-07:00","id":186711,"title":"","content":"Way too much for me to respond to while at work, but I'll throw in an observation. While I was playing WW online there was far too much potential for crossover to ignore. Some systems played better with others in the sandbox and some didn't. Those people trying to play Abominations or Dhampires, were few and far between and largely ignored un;ess they were just that damn good of a writer they could either make the whole thing plausible and suspend disbelief or were playing with others that were just as cream-filled spongecake as they were. As an ST I was faced with crossovers of a different sort like the Mage who was a Glasswalker Kinfolk and trying to earn the right to have a place in the Caern. Now that kind of crossing over I can get behind because it brings a bit more of an unknown dynamic to the table. Granted, at the time I was a novice and I didn't know the mage system well enough and the player ran roughshod over our game and then snubbed his nose at our group when enough of the other players and characters got annoyed with his behavior both IC and OOC and kicked him out. It's players like that which prompt STs to have a <u>No Crossover At All</u> rule partially for having to learn another system and the headache of having to deal with the clashing cosmology behind both when the characters get into it over some belief or another. I have however seen STs running Tabletop who know several systems like the back of their hand and can handle having the Lost cub, Pooka, Mage/kinfolk, Ghoul play party running around their city of darkness of choice and still manage to keep it flowing well and all the different rules straight. I think mainly the choice for having any kind of crossover in game depends on how well the ST is prepared for it and how long they can keep up with shenanigans between characters before burning out on making it all work and be fun for everyone.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-03T10:28:04-07:00","id":187237,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerushad</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/186219-on-snowflakes-and-golden-rules?p=186619#post186619\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One bone of contention I have (more like a pet peeve) is the very concept of powergamers... ...But what about those megalomaniacal sociopaths who really are willing to violate taboo and become powermongers. The character can't be called a powergamer, because to them it's no game, it's their life and their master plan to gain the corrupted powers of the world just like Sammy H did</div> </div> </div> </div>I *seriously* question the motivations of ANY player who creates this kind of character. The &quot;I'm not a powergamer, I'm just playing a *character* who's a powermonger!!!&quot; excuse has been used by every single egregious powergamer min-max tweaker I've ever played with.<br /> <br /> Creating a character who's a &quot;powermonger by nature&quot; is simply the shallow excuse that powergamers use to get away with abusing the system. You have to question WHY the *player* deliberately created a powermonger character in the first place. 9 times out of 10 it's to collect &quot;kewl powrz&quot;. It's not like the characters are assigned randomly, the player creates the character concept. You can't blame your powergaming on having a powermonger character - you *created* the character!<br /> <br /> Yes, there are megalomaniacal sociopaths out there, and yes players should be free to build whatever kind of character they like, but any player who's concept is &quot;megalomaniacal sociopathic powermonger&quot; is going to get some serious side-eye from the other players and should likely receive a strong talking to by the ST about what role-playing games are supposed to be about.<br /> <br /> All of this goes back to your question about combinations/abominations ==&gt; I've never seen a character pursue this who *wasn't* driven by a powergamer trying to &quot;win&quot; by getting the most dots on their sheet.<br /> <br /> As for questioning &quot;the very concept of powergamers&quot;, I assure you they DO exist. One of the members of my old gaming group was actually *proud* of his min-maxing! (He *always* played Tremere!) So much so that one day he showed me the spreadsheet he had built to tweak his characters during to creation ensure he was getting the most &quot;bang for his buck&quot; - the most &quot;dot value&quot; for the least creation/freebie points. He had every single merit and flaw published at the time incorporated into it - if you wanted to increase some stat during creation, he could just punch in some numbers and tell you the cheapest way to max it out. If that's not a concrete example of powergaming, then I'm a flying pig.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1815-joeduncan","name":"JoeDuncan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-03T12:12:18-07:00","id":187300,"title":"","content":"You can also play a egotistical power hungry narcissist/megalomaniac without jumping into snowflake concepts, crossover character types, or out of type rare powers.<br /> <br /> That's basically the MO of a few canonical groups as it is.<br /> <br /> For example, if you're playing a Hermetic mage from House Tytalus, there's an expectation of being very concerned with your personal power. Lots of Tytalans end up dabbling in things like vampire powers. However this is seen as a failure from their particular power-hungry philosophy because you're trying to take the easy way to power, meaning you haven't earned it and are subverting your own Supreme Will.<br /> <br /> Playing a powermonger doesn't mean you don't understand the risk of certain sources of power and don't want to risk them. Why spend a year learning a type of magic vampires can't usually accesses when you could just socially manipulate a cult of mortal magicians to do the magic for you?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-03T13:46:07-07:00","id":187381,"title":"","content":"I agree, you can play an egotistical power hungry so-and-so who does things a different way. What I don't understand is why the other way isn't also valid, even if improbable. If a person who discovers some new kind of power would take interest in it, I want to know the reasons that they can or can't and how it would work. The usual solution of keeping the venues separate is fine, I understand that it's the default assumption. My objective is to find out what is actually impossible and why. What I really want is to explore the possibilities of how it would work if someone did these things. &quot;If,&quot; not &quot;should.&quot; I know this is an unpopular opinion, and I accept that. Again, I'm not trying to upset anyone, I'm only trying to figure out one of many options for playing an admittedly unique variety of character concepts, and for no better reason than &quot;it's there.&quot; Like Kirk and the mountain. <br /> <br /> Though I understand the trepidation of an ST who has a player wanting to play this concept, I must respectfully challenge the preconception that they are automatically up to no good. If the player is cooperative with the ST and other players, courteous of the kind of story that they are trying to tell, and puts in the effort to make the kind of character he wants to play, I believe that he should be rewarded for his effort and respected like any other player. It is a game, and the rules are only there to mediate disputes, not to keep people from having fun or being creative. There are so many rejoinders to this that I can already hear coming: &quot;Play a different game&quot; or &quot;just use the golden rule&quot;, but those answers don't satisfy me. I want to use this system, I want to be able to validate a certain view instead of waving ST fiat around like a magic wand. I'm looking for the facts from the canon of the shared mythos we've all grown to love. Like Beckett, scouring the globe for truth. (Though admittedly without such a cool jacket!)<br /> <br /> To answer Heavy Arms: Your last paragraph about the powermonger manipulating someone else into doing it instead: In that position, I would venture to say, &quot;If you want something done right, you have to do it yourself.&quot; That's the usual trap villains fall into: Make the minion do it. But if you have the power yourself, you have the ability to enact change with it and you don't have to worry about losing it. And now you're more potent against the forces of your own venue that will come for you expecting normal powers typical to your type. It's misdirection, a trump card, an ace up your sleeve. Or it isn't. Or you do it the way you suggested. Whatever you choose to do. It's all about options. It's not a popular option, or the easiest, or even the best, but it is an option. I want to know how the option would work if someone did choose it.<br /> <br /> In reply to JoeDuncan: While I respect your right to seriously question their motivations, I must insist upon &quot;innocent until proven guilty&quot; if only in regard to this theoretical situation. I realize there are powergamers, but to automatically brand someone is elitist and very unkind to someone who is just trying to play a game the way they want to. Your player who is good at running numbers sounds to me like he has a skill that he's proud of. Admittedly, I don't know him, and I'm sure you have reason to be annoyed with his antics, but my knee-jerk reaction is &quot;good for him if he was clever and wants recognition for it.&quot; I feel there is nothing wrong with what he's doing until he uses it to unfair advantage and victimizes the other players or ST, at which point the mathematical skill isn't bad, the application of it is. So, if they're a known problem player, I understand they need to be handled accordingly for the enjoyment of everyone in the group, and I agree with you entirely. However, I'm talking about people who aren't just in it for a power trip or to boss other players around. I'm talking about people who would be responsible with these wildly unpopular options.<br /> <br /> I once had this conversation with a fellow ST, and he asked me to describe how a character would go about it. I answered honestly with my usual ideas and then awaited a thoughtful reply. To my chagrin, he told me that no ST in their right minds would allow it to succeed. I commented that, in the absence of ST fiat, that there was no reason to say it couldn't happen. His reply was an irate &quot;There's no reason it could either.&quot; And, yes, he was right, but so was I. Chance doesn't always favor the bold, but it could. If that's not the kind of game you want to run, okay. But it is the kind of game I want to run. I want to do it well and I want to do it right. Again, I want to know how it could, not why it shouldn't. Everything you've said is valid from one point of view. I want to see it from the other, too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3407-cerushad","name":"Cerushad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3407_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-03T21:48:17-07:00","id":187711,"title":"","content":"Also I would like to note that htere is considerable difference playing a power monger character and being a powerplayer. For example in our current years long Aberrant game my character erupted as an Angel, Supernaturally beautiful, Charismatic with a few light effects, supernatural resistance to damage and teleportation abilities. Now years later the character has become a major frontline player for Teragen, he has survived through more battles than he would ever have wanted to be involved in and knows there will be major conflicts ahead, he is, partially because of his powers and partially because that is who he is, rather self-centered and as a Terat Marvel consciously seek to guide his own evolution into something greater and stronger. He is very much a power hungry character, yet I as a player have consciously avoided buying any straight out combat powers to him. He still brings into battle mundane firearms, even though he himself and many of the enemies he faces are all but immune to mere bullets, and so tends to win his battles through clever side-ways thinking and sometimes through perseverance and luck. It has been only now years later that I got my first power that could be used in an straight out attack power, because it is a power set that includes such in it's techniques I still avoided taking any such technique meaning that should I ever be tempted to use it in such a manner it would cost horribly in terms of Quantum points to use in such a manner. And even now the possibility exists only because such a Technique is part of the power set I bought, it was not the reason for the decision to get it I got the power because it was logical natural way for his powers to develop. So what I am saying that there is a way to play a power mongering character without being a min-maxing power player. By making IC logical decision in ways which your character grows even though you as a player OOCly know they are inneficient ways to develop your character compared to his goals and opposition.<br /> <br /> Also as for the Vampire Mage concept. You can easily execute this without the need for a crossover, both in Mage and in Vampire. In Vampire, sorry if I sound a bit ostentatious, this is basically resolved by playing a Tremere. That is what the Tremere literally are, there is no need to add Awakened Magic in to it, especially since the backstory has seven Archmages, not to mention their Master class apprentices etc, basically having struggled for centuries trying to get their magic back and Thaumaturgy is the best they could achieve. In Mage there are a variety of Merits that allow you to build your Vampire Mage. There is a Merit for Fangs, Flaw for adding weakness to Sunlight, Flaw for adding a Beast to your mage giving his a chance of Frenzy etc. It all can be done here by using your freebies thoughtfully. Yes it generally means you don't have enough to necessarily min-max your character but you weren't after min-maxing right? You wanted to have a unique character concept, then you have to pay the points for having such a unique situation. This also tends to reveal whether it was a unique concept a player was after or just an effort to min-max his character into some Sam Haigh copy attempt.<br /> <br /> Naturally my opinions only, YMMV.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-03T23:12:59-07:00","id":187748,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerushad</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/186219-on-snowflakes-and-golden-rules?p=187381#post187381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I don't understand is why the other way isn't also valid, even if improbable.</div> </div> </div> </div>It isn't always; at least as far as things go in-character. What gets discouraged out-of-character is stuff that is disruptive. Part of this is that the games are written from the perspective of catering to a group of players. A game with four normal vampire PCs and one Sam Haight quickly turns into a game with one Sam Haight and four bored people.<br /> <br /> Even when there are in-universe explanations and repercussions (like the effect of vitae on mages) one player going down that path pretty much drags the whole group along with the ride because the group will have to deal with it.<br /> <br /> That's also why WW/OP have always stressed the Golden Rule of changing the rules if it makes your game more fun.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm only trying to figure out one of many options for playing an admittedly unique variety of character concepts, and for no better reason than &quot;it's there.&quot; </div> </div> </div>I think what gets most of us is that, in the end, these aren't really unique concepts. They're concepts that were already actually available without going into these extreme edge cases in the fiction of the setting.<br /> <br /> You don't need a Ghoul-Mage to have a vampire magician. We have Tremere for that.<br /> <br /> You don't need Hedge Magic to make a Garou/Fera with ritual magical tricks beyond their Gifts. We have Rites for that.<br /> <br /> Etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I want to use this system, I want to be able to validate a certain view instead of waving ST fiat around like a magic wand. </div> </div> </div>To a certain extent... you're going to be disappointed because the authors of the game never intended to make some of these things as having set answers in the books. The only validation is going to be the books telling you, as the ST, to apply ST judgement.<br /> <br /> This is even more true when you're talking about mixing in stuff from other White Wolf games like Trinity-verse or Exalted. You're not going to find canonical validation of mixing things from unrelated universes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To answer Heavy Arms: Your last paragraph about the powermonger manipulating someone else into doing it instead: In that position, I would venture to say, &quot;If you want something done right, you have to do it yourself.&quot; That's the usual trap villains fall into: Make the minion do it. </div> </div> </div>That's a fiction trope yes. But not one that's built into the rules. If anything the WOD (the cWOD especially of the various ones) is very heavily built around making this an extremely effective tactic.<br /> <br /> The &quot;you have to do it yourself&quot; thing is also fairly easy to dismiss from a reality based perspective. We might remember the names of the leaders, but I assure you no famous historical leader got their without having &quot;minions.&quot; Knowing how to effectively delegate work is pretty much a defining feature of successful leaders. Sure. Some generals lead from the front instead of the tent. Some presidents were/are very hands on in managing their staff/cabinet/etc. But they all invariably have to delegate because numbers are one of the biggest powers in our world and organizing them takes a team of people.<br /> <br /> In the WOD you don't have to do it yourself. The rules fully support having competent minions that do things for you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-04T01:29:07-07:00","id":187773,"title":"","content":"<br /> I would respectfully challenge everyone who speaks out against so-called powergaming and min-maxing to find a valid, non-paradoxical, logical reason why spending one's points in an effective way is immoral in a game that has a combat system. If the ST states that the setting doesn't need that, or says he wants very normal characters or something else to start with, okay, it's not good to front load combat stuff. I get that. But in a more freeform setting where you could design a character however you wish, what *exactly* is wrong with spending your points well? Do we not put points in Medicine if we play a doctor? Do we not put dots in Academics if we play a scholar? If you want to fight, put dots in fighting abilities, spend your merits on it, use WoD: Combat! If you want to bridge the gap between disparate supernatural races a la Combined: the Accord or something similar, why not learn some of their powers to better understand them? Ambassadors learn the languages of the places they visit, learn their customs, use their greetings and forms of respect. It's no different, *when and if* it is setting appropriate. I feel that it just comes down to the kind of game you're walking into. It's either appropriate for the setting or it isn't. These aren't bad people for wanting to be good at the game. The labels are superfluous and unkind. <br /> <br /> For Heavy Arms: I understand and agree with everything you're saying. All of those are completely valid points. *However*: It isn't about need, it's about want. Sure, you don't need to reinvent the wheel, but some times the new wheel you make gives you a good feeling, because it's your wheel the way you like it. Invalidating the &quot;special-ness&quot; of the concept or the originality doesn't change that it's all there and can be put together. I'm trying to figure out how, not asking permission to do so. It's about knowledge. I get what you're saying, but I still wish to pursue understanding of these things. It isn't hurting anyone, otherwise I wouldn't do it. It's just my own personal little project; I don't plan on using anything discussed here to strong-arm an ST into allowing something they don't want in their games. That's a quick way for me to get kicked out. And yes, it would tend to drag everyone into it, so I would prefer to be in a group that was already intending to get into this stuff. Birds of a feather, etc. The rules may have been built in certain ways, but by many different writers, and for many unknowable reasons. After the tragedy that was Exalted 2.5 I do not place much faith in the *design* of the rules. Even if I did, it only means that what I propose is more challenging, not invalidated by the rules in any way. Right now all we're doing is waxing semantic; I'd really like to get back to the meat of the discussion and find some of the neat little clues that are hidden around the books. <br /> <br /> For me: Anyway. With regard to my original topic: I know that the *easiest* way to cross lines is to reforge the Conquistador's Sword (maybe as a fetish tattoo on one's fists) and go a-hunting. But after the Skinner's plan, it seems to lack a certain finesse. What I'm curious about is the academics behind the concept. *How* does the sword do what it does? It breaks paradigm, it breaks rules. It's a Plot Device, yes, but it functions in some definable way in-world. (I'm guessing Arch-Spirit.)<br /> <br /> How would you go about replicating that in other ways, for the sake of experimentation? One of my past concepts involved broken shards of the sword granting only access to certain abilities, or only to one or two dots of ability. Though a little off topic, I once played a Mokol\u00e9 who made a tattoo version of the Bowl of Mnesis on his brow and eventually became Dumenkara after eating too many brains. My players, a group of hardened Archons, were freshly horrified by seeing their contacts and allies with bitten-open skulls and took the monster down very adeptly with the help of a local Sept and Chantry. They even helped some Ronin form a Unity Caern afterward, with a bit of help from a Nagaraja blood bonding the spirits to cooperate. My group enjoyed it, everyone had fun, we moved on to other things. The world didn't end. Well, not for another year, lol. This game was of course many years ago, before Gehenna or the nWoD Accord venue came out, so I'm glad to see them embracing cross-venue cooperation, even if it isn't for my preferred World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> I digress. Any ideas or thoughts? <br /> <br /> *Can* Garou still learn Hedge Magic, or keep the Numina they had before the First Change? If not, what happens to the dots? Do they get a refund like old Sorcerer suggested? And if not, why, if the Bastet and Kitsune can?<br /> <br /> My wife loves playing Dark Ages Fae, what about a Fianna Kinain with Dominions, how could that work? One Fianna camp already has fae blood, albeit from more modern Changelings, not Firstborn or Old Changelings. Seems ripe for crossover chicanery to me. <br /> <br /> And if a Mokol\u00e9 ever forsook the Sun, renouncing him in some arcane way, could he then be Embraced without the gruesome side effects? If a Kitsune is Embraced, but is immune to fire, do they still go up in the Blood-Fire Death? What would someone have to do to sever that bond? Irritate a Celestine?<br /> <br /> And yes, I want to know consequences as much as potential.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3407-cerushad","name":"Cerushad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3407_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-04T07:56:59-07:00","id":187990,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerushad</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/186219-on-snowflakes-and-golden-rules?p=187773#post187773\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would respectfully challenge everyone who speaks out against so-called powergaming and min-maxing to find a valid, non-paradoxical, logical reason why spending one's points in an effective way is immoral in a game that has a combat system.</div> </div> </div> </div>The existence of a combat system is irrelevant.<br /> <br /> The &quot;immorality&quot; of problematic game-play styles is ultimately that they violate the social contract that is part of any gaming group. While each group is going to be different, every group (either explicitly or implicitly) will have a set of standards on how much tolerance they have for putting systems over fiction. The contract exists because RPGs are a group activity and only truly function when everyone is having fun. Violating that contract is &quot;immoral&quot; because it inherently means valuing one person's fun over impact that might have on the rest of the group's fun.<br /> <br /> This applies to pretty much any term used in this sort of fashion; about players or the GM/ST/etc. or even the authors of the books.<br /> <br /> This does mean that there is never a universally applicable definition of terms like powergaming or min-maxing because such behaviors are always tolerable to an extent. The problem lies in violating social norms by taking any given behavior too far for the group's tastes.<br /> <br /> Spending your points effectively becomes problematic when your emphasis on that particular motivation exceeds the group norm and then becomes powergaming or min-maxing or whatever. Consider these things &quot;crimes&quot; of extremes, not absolutes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> *However*: It isn't about need, it's about want. </div> </div> </div>Personal want always needs to be balanced against the want of the group in a group social activity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sure, you don't need to reinvent the wheel, but some times the new wheel you make gives you a good feeling, because it's your wheel the way you like it. </div> </div> </div>In my experience such pleasure is short lived and largely less fun than anyone claims it is. The most enjoyment comes not out of re-inventing the wheel to feeling neat about it - since that feeling is fleeting as it only really applies to making the wheel - but of taking a stock wheel and modifying it for your personal tastes and style so that it will always be your personal take on things. What makes a character unique and special in a good way is not what mechanical widgets you can shoehorn into their sheet, but what you as a creative individual bring to the concept to make it stand out in a new and interesting way. A special widget might be part of making that happen, but the widgets are tools, not the expression of your creativity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm trying to figure out how, not asking permission to do so. </div> </div> </div>As I said though, &quot;how&quot; is ultimately not something you're going to find answers for, for many of these questions. The books don't have them. They were never meant to have them. If we're talking about things the books present as interesting ideas, they are often not given a how explicitly because the exist as ideas for you to explore how over the course of a game if you're interested in it rather than letting you come out of the gates with the answer.<br /> <br /> If you want to run a game about trying to recreate a magical item from the books that was supposedly unique, having the books give you the answers makes that a pretty boring game. It also validates only one approach to doing it. Having it be a mystery lets you run the game in dozens of different ways that can all be equally valid and entertaining.<br /> <br /> We, as a community, can at best offer &quot;how&quot; we would handle such things. But you have accept that for many of us the answer is, &quot;I wouldn't do that for X reasons.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-04T11:54:55-07:00","id":188166,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerushad</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/186219-on-snowflakes-and-golden-rules?p=187381#post187381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...I must insist upon &quot;innocent until proven guilty&quot;...</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey, that's great in a legal context but, to put it bluntly, idiotic in this one. <br /> <br /> If when someone yells &quot;Think fast!&quot; at me, 9 times out of 10 they immediately punch me in the face, you have zero right to tell me I'm not allowed to pre-emptively bring up my guard every time someone yells &quot;Think fast!&quot; at me. Same thing here. And I was being generously conservative with the 9/10 figure. The fact of the matter is, I have *NEVER* seen a player make a powermonger character who wasn't an egregious powergamer. I would have said 100% of the time, but I was trying to give you the benefit of the doubt.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerushad</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/186219-on-snowflakes-and-golden-rules?p=187381#post187381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...automatically brand someone is elitist and very unkind to someone who is just trying to play a game the way they want to...</div> </div> </div> </div>Straw man. I didn't say to automatically brand them as a powergamer, I said STs should be highly suspicious and have a good sit-down talk with the player.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerushad</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/186219-on-snowflakes-and-golden-rules?p=187381#post187381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...nothing wrong with what he's doing until he uses it to unfair advantage and victimizes the other players or ST...</div> </div> </div> </div>Which, again, is something I've never seen this kind of character NOT do. You'll excuse me if I'm skeptical, but every time I've seen a player make a powermonger character, the whole point was to do exactly what you say above (and thus &quot;win&quot; in their minds I guess?).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerushad</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/186219-on-snowflakes-and-golden-rules?p=187381#post187381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...I would respectfully challenge everyone who speaks out against so-called powergaming and min-maxing to find a valid, non-paradoxical, logical reason why spending one's points in an effective way is immoral...</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem with blatant min-maxing is that the character is then making life decisions using information they can't actually possess. *Characters* know *nothing* about the rules or the system. Only the player does. Quick, which is the most efficient way to become good at hand to hand combat in real life:<br /> <br /> a) taking martial arts lessons as a kid<br /> b) growing up in Okinawa as the child of a Sensei<br /> c) making a dedicated study of martial arts as an adult<br /> <br /> I don't know, you don't know, no one knows. Maybe it's none of them and a fourth option is best. Deliberately min-maxing your character is akin to having extra-universal information as a child and knowing in advance which option would give you the best fighting skills for the least effort. Of course it's impossible as a player to NOT have this information, but deliberately abusing it by building or playing your character as though *they* did is *entirely* contra to the whole spirit of playing an RPG. You're supposed to at least *try* (in good faith) to maintain the separation between player and character knowledge. That's why it's wrong: it breaks the fourth wall.<br /> <br /> I also don't buy the &quot;I'm just curious as to what's possible&quot; routine either. Again, this is a tack that I have seen used by every powergamer I've ever played with. They wheedle away at the ST with &quot;well what about this?&quot;, &quot;what about that?&quot;, or &quot;what would happen if I did this and that together?&quot;. Yet, every single time I have seen an ST get duped into believing the player was &quot;just sincerely curious&quot;, the player has immediately turned around and abused it as fully as possible. I've never seen a player use the &quot;I'm just curious&quot; routine with an ST who *actually was* just curious: it's *always* been a smoke screen ploy to extract game breaking admissions/rulings/info from the ST.<br /> <br /> The best ST I ever had handled these types of situations by saying something like: &quot;That's not information your character would know, so I'm not going to tell you. If you want to know, try it in-game and see&quot;. And then if the player *did* try whatever bullshit they were hoping to do, they mostly wound up dead. On rare occasions he would allow it, but he was pretty good at discouraging and preventing the most OP gamebreaking BS.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1815-joeduncan","name":"JoeDuncan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-04T16:43:14-07:00","id":188300,"title":"","content":"Heavy Arms: Thank you for the constructive input! I completely agree with what you said about these being crimes of extremes -- that was the exact point I've been trying to illustrate. I don't seem to always express myself effectively; You stated it very succinctly and fairly. I appreciate it. <br /> <br /> I understand that everyone here has already hashed this out, heard the perspective, and had bad experiences. I wasn't here for that, and I want to know what people thought. I know that there are no actual answers to &quot;how&quot;, but I want to hear those ideas people have! That's exactly what I'm looking for! Tell me how you did it, tell me what you think about it, reference that little-known quote from the back of some obscure book, quote an author's reply on a message board, show me a connection you made between two seemingly-different topics, let's brainstorm! It's the Da Vinci Code up in here! lol I know that there will be a certain amount of disappointment from the official sources, but it's the fandom input I'd like to explore when canon is exhausted. <br /> <br /> For Joe: Straw man? I'm sorry, that was a misunderstanding, not my intention; I'm not trying to discredit you or what you're saying. I even agree that the ST should thoroughly investigate before allowing it. The intent of what I was saying is this: I'm unhappy about people being unkind to each other over styles of gameplay, which are ultimately *personal preference*, which by definition *cannot be wrong*, (especially in like-minded company, lol!) Everyone can have opinions, obviously, but even the opinions and feelings of a &quot;powergamer&quot; are valid. Even if they are just being jerks, I feel they need correction, not derision. This is a recreational activity, I find no call for hostility. If they have a group that enjoys it and they don't, as Heavy Arms said, violate the social contract, then they're not wrong. I thank you for giving me the benefit of the doubt, because it is that theoretical 1 out of 10 that I'm defending. Please rest assured that I, like you, have no love of players who just want to wave their metaphorical this-and-that around and make everyone else miserable. I do, however, try to give everyone the benefit of the doubt. The real problem I see is people behaving the wrong way for the wrong type of group. Everything else, all the trappings of my posts up until this point have been to say *that* and to start a brainstorm about a topic that I find interesting, in my own opinion. I welcome those of similar taste to join me.<br /> <br /> I'm not idiotic, friend; I'm optimistic. I am not throwing out any kind of &quot;routine&quot;, I'm bring very genuine and cooperative here. If it sounds like I'm being deceitful, that's only because of the other 9 out of 10 who have ruined it for everyone else. If I have sounded angry, it's really that I'm irate about the concepts which I find abhorrent, not angry with any person involved in this conversation. I will try to keep my tone more level so we can return to being civil with one another. <br /> <br /> It sounds like your ST had the right idea. I like that approach. The ST needs to be able to handle whatever the players throw at him. (That challenge is part of the fun, for me.) I prefer to allow the powergaming, and then illustrate through IC hardships why it was a Bad Idea (TM). Then they have to live with a bad decision, and spend their precious XP on fixing it. Or go lose the Wolf, get Gilgulled, etc to get their points back and try again. It makes a very potent lasting impression, and leaves scars on the character to remind the player not to repeat the mistakes of the past. <br /> <br /> Thank you for accepting my challenge and providing a very valid case against min-maxing. I agree, and you actually gave me an interesting idea for a character. A grandfather who is Shih, and wants to prepare his recently orphaned grandson for the eventual battle with the Kuei who killed his parents. He takes him into the mountains, trains him from a young child to be a living weapon, but in doing so he becomes the true monster, denying the boy a chance at a normal life. The kid develops fugue states, and even after walking away from the dojo he ends up hurting people when startled. How does he live in a world he knows nothing about? How does he live with the power he's been given? Then the super-warrior has to go about exploring the mundane world, where his fighting skills mean nothing and are effectively useless. (Though even if someone did use this concept for power, it provides a more palatable IC reason why the character was built with such a design: A mentor with an agenda is not extra-universal knowledge if handled responsibly. Key word: Responsibly. If it's not setting appropriate, yes, it's fourth-wall breaking. If you're playing a Hakken Ahroun in a Hengeyokai setting, this could be a fun concept.)<br /> <br /> So, Garou and Hedge Magic? Do they keep it or lose it?<br /> <br /> What would you do to represent a Kinain blooded by Dark Ages Changelings with their Mists, Weaving, and Dominions? <br /> <br /> And Hsien Kinain would be a great system to use to run a game in the world of Avatar/Korra using the Storyteller system! But would it be viable in the CWoD? If not, why?<br /> <br /> Under the (knowingly incorrect) assumption that the CWoD and Exalted were actually still connected: Do you think that all Dragons of the WoD descended from the Dragon Kings, or only the Mokol\u00e9, with the others being evolved River Dragons or something like that?<br /> <br /> Any thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3407-cerushad","name":"Cerushad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3407_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-04T16:55:15-07:00","id":188313,"title":"","content":"To Rose: I'm sorry, your post got muddled with the others and I didn't see it! And what a coincidence, the concept of the Kinfolk Mage trying to find acceptance with his changer family is actually the concept I'm playing right now! You have my sympathy for the player's bad behavior, I've had a lot of that with a few of mine, too. If they're going to play something special, the least they could do is be polite and helpful about it, not snobbish. That kind of attitude from them certainly seems to be the source of all of my grief. <br /> <br /> To Possessed: The idea of a mage whose paradigm is vampiric is delicious. I'm very interested in exploring that! What are these merits called? I'd love to run a plot about a vampire who Awakened only to have the players find out it was just an Awakened who thinks he's a vampire! <br /> <br /> Also, I like your approach to discouraging powergaming. There is definitely a certain honor in making less &quot;efficient&quot; choices that are based on the actual gameplay. I would actually love to do away with the XP system entirely and just let the ST issue improvements based on the things the players did that session! (If the players didn't mind not being able to spend their own XP. I've had issues with that in the past, and when I tried to lighten their load and roll all the dice behind the screen they didn't seem to take kindly to it. Ah well, to each his own I guess. Still, it would be so much purer an acting experience if they would let me. I think maybe they don't trust me to give them what they want... Not sure what to think about that.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3407-cerushad","name":"Cerushad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3407_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-04T22:16:18-07:00","id":188511,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerushad</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/186219-on-snowflakes-and-golden-rules?p=188313#post188313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> To Possessed: The idea of a mage whose paradigm is vampiric is delicious. I'm very interested in exploring that! What are these merits called? I'd love to run a plot about a vampire who Awakened only to have the players find out it was just an Awakened who thinks he's a vampire!</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends on the Merit/Flaw and most of the useful ones in this regard can be found in Guide to the Traditions. I think the one that grants Fangs (retractable if you pay a bit extra) was called Natural Weaponry and it could grant Fangs/Claws/Horns/Tail in any combination and with or without them being retractable. It get's expensive fast but can be used to round up quite a few different concepts. Also the same book has the Weakness Flaw which allows you to define a weakness for your character the deadliness of which depends upon the points it grants. There was also Strong Pattern Merit, or something on those lines can't remember the names, that made your pattern more resistant to damage and a Flaw the name of which eludes me that makes you susceptible to Frenzy. It also includes the Immunity Merit that scales in cost up to and until costing the full 21 freebies a character can have for being damn near immortal. Despite it's possibility for abuse if used well with ST guidance it can be used to complement certain character concepts. Generally though the Merits/Flaws of Mage (core+supplements) along with Backgrounds and adversarial Backgrounds can be used to built almost any concept from those Mages who are barely touched by the supernatural apart from their magic to those who belong to bloodlines infused with supernatural blood for centuries.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerushad</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/186219-on-snowflakes-and-golden-rules?p=188313#post188313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, I like your approach to discouraging powergaming. There is definitely a certain honor in making less &quot;efficient&quot; choices that are based on the actual gameplay. I would actually love to do away with the XP system entirely and just let the ST issue improvements based on the things the players did that session! (If the players didn't mind not being able to spend their own XP. I've had issues with that in the past, and when I tried to lighten their load and roll all the dice behind the screen they didn't seem to take kindly to it. Ah well, to each his own I guess. Still, it would be so much purer an acting experience if they would let me. I think maybe they don't trust me to give them what they want... Not sure what to think about that.)</div> </div> </div> </div>We've been using a bit different XP system for years, close to ten years or so, where the ST grants exp according to what the character has been up to. That is you get exp directly to abilities you have used/studied in game and generally one point in a more general category that can then be used almost anywhere. If using the specific XP to raise the stat it was given to you need to ST input, it already came in the form of you being granted those XP points, but to use the XP from general category to raise something else you need ST input to justify why would you qualify for that stat raise ICly.<br /> Also I encourage players to remember that the character have no OOC information and ability to determine the most efficient direction of development. A matter which has brought me into conflict with the rest of the Exalted fanbase in numerous occasions since there, on the forums I might note not around any table I have sat on, it has been more of the norm to choose your charms because you OOCly have knowledge of what's available in the books everywhere and only building the most efficient characters possible. Their reasoning being that to do it in any other way is just idiotic, but they forget that Exaltation does not bring with it omniscience of all the abilities Exalted can possess and thus it should be more of the norm for them, the characters that is, to develop the Charms that grow most naturally to them based on personalities and interests instead of Paranoia combat builds. Naturally YMMV.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-05T00:10:39-07:00","id":188580,"title":"","content":"I like that use-based approach to XP. It reminds me of Morrowind, easily my favorite video game ever. I think I'll give that a try the next time I run game, thanks for all of the information! I must have thumbed through that book dozens of times and never noticed how versatile those merits can be for building unique concepts. Thanks for pointing them out! Now I want to make an Akashayana with a monkey tail based on Sun Wukong, lol!<br /> <br /> I wish they hadn't cancelled the MMO, it would have been so nice to play in this world while leaving the system mechanics under the hood. Exalted Paranoia combat ruined the game for me. I ended up just writing stories based in the setting instead of running game because it was just more fun than dealing with it. There were so many truly mythic things to explore, it's such a waste to focus all that effort on just being invincible to fight other Exalted. I mean, really, in a world with a population larger than ours, and only 900+ Celestial, and 10,000+ Terrestrial, with a landmass larger than Earth's, there has to be somewhere to go where you won't have to fight a battle won in two ticks. And the only real threat is from other player characters! All of the canon NPCs aren't that strong! I'd rather build an empire in Exalted than topple one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3407-cerushad","name":"Cerushad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3407_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}